{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-344166",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $q(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${q}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,9),$ and ${q}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $q$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(9) & =-1 \\\\ q(1) & =q(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{1}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =q(9)-{\\int }_{1}^{9}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1-12 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $q$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $q$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,-13\\}=-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $q$ is ${q}_{\\text{min}}=-13,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $q(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
